    "summary": "The Supreme Court held that in a contract made through an instantaneous mode of communication, such as telephone, the contract is complete when the acceptance is received by the offeror. The Court distinguished telephone communication from acceptance through post, where different rules apply.",
    "facts": [
      "The plaintiffs, situated in Ahmedabad, made an offer over the telephone to purchase cottonseed cake from the defendants, who were in Khamgaon.",
      "The defendants accepted the offer over the telephone.",
      "A dispute arose when the defendants failed to supply the goods.",
      "The plaintiffs filed a suit in Ahmedabad seeking compensation for breach of contract.",
      "The defendants argued that the Ahmedabad court had no jurisdiction because the acceptance had taken place in Khamgaon.",
      "The main question was where a contract made through a telephone conversation is considered to be complete."
    ],
    "decision": [
      "The Supreme Court held that in a contract made through an instantaneous mode of communication, such as telephone, the contract is complete when the acceptance is received by the offeror.",
      "The Court distinguished telephone communication from acceptance through post, where different rules apply.",
      "Since the acceptance was heard by the plaintiffs in Ahmedabad, the contract was completed there.",
      "The Ahmedabad court therefore had jurisdiction to entertain the suit.",
      "The case established the rule regarding the place and time of formation of contracts through instantaneous communication."
    ],
