    "citation": "(1903) 30 IA 114 (PC); AIR 1903 PC 1",
    "subject": "Indian Contract Act, 1872",
    "subjectSlug": "contract-act",
    "principle": "",
    "summary": "The Privy Council held that an agreement entered into by a minor is void from the beginning (void ab initio). A minor is not competent to enter into a contract under Section 11 of the Indian Contract Act.",
    "facts": [
      "Dharmodas Ghose was a minor when he mortgaged his property to a moneylender, Brahmo Dutt, to secure a loan of ₹20,000.",
      "The mortgage was executed through the moneylender’s attorney.",
      "The attorney was aware that Dharmodas was a minor, even though the mortgage documents contained a declaration that he had attained majority.",
      "Later, Dharmodas filed a suit seeking cancellation of the mortgage, arguing that he was a minor when he entered into the transaction.",
      "The moneylender argued that the minor had misrepresented his age and should therefore be prevented from denying the validity of the mortgage.",
      "The moneylender also sought repayment of the amount advanced."
    ],
    "decision": [
      "The Privy Council held that an agreement entered into by a minor is void from the beginning (void ab initio).",
      "A minor is not competent to enter into a contract under Section 11 of the Indian Contract Act.",
      "The doctrine of estoppel could not be used to make the minor liable because the moneylender’s agent already knew about his minority.",
      "The minor could not be compelled to repay the loan under the ordinary contractual provisions relating to restitution.",
      "The judgment established that a minor’s agreement is void and cannot be enforced against the minor."
    ],
