      "The destruction of the music hall made performance impossible because the continued existence of the hall was essential to the agreement.",
      "The parties had contracted on the assumption that the hall would continue to exist.",
      "Since the destruction occurred without the fault of either party, the contract was discharged.",
      "The case established the common-law doctrine of frustration of contract due to destruction of the subject matter."
