    "summary": "The House of Lords held that Dunlop could not enforce the agreement against Selfridge. There was no direct contractual relationship between Dunlop and Selfridge.",
    "facts": [
      "Dunlop sold tyres to Dew & Co. under an agreement that the goods would not be resold below the prescribed list price.",
      "Dew & Co. further sold the tyres to Selfridge & Co.",
      "Selfridge agreed in writing not to sell the tyres below the fixed price and promised to pay damages to Dunlop if it violated the agreement.",
      "Selfridge later sold the tyres below the agreed price.",
      "Dunlop filed a suit against Selfridge seeking damages for breach of contract.",
      "Selfridge argued that Dunlop could not sue because there was no direct contract between them."
    ],
    "decision": [
      "The House of Lords held that Dunlop could not enforce the agreement against Selfridge.",
      "There was no direct contractual relationship between Dunlop and Selfridge.",
      "The Court reaffirmed the doctrine of privity of contract, according to which generally only parties to a contract can sue to enforce its terms.",
      "It also emphasised that a person seeking to enforce a contract must generally show that consideration was provided by them or through an authorised agent.",
      "The case is an important authority on the doctrine of privity of contract."
    ],
