    "principle": "",
    "summary": "The Supreme Court held that the contract had not become impossible to perform merely because the Government had temporarily requisitioned a portion of the land. The requisition was temporary in nature and did not destroy the fundamental basis of the contract.",
    "facts": [
      "Mugneeram Bangur & Co. owned a large area of land near Calcutta and started a scheme to develop it into residential plots.",
      "The company entered into agreements with purchasers for the sale of plots and received earnest money.",
      "The company was also responsible for constructing roads and drains to make the plots suitable for residential purposes.",
      "Satyabrata Ghose entered into an agreement to purchase a plot and paid ₹101 as earnest money.",
      "Before the development work could be completed, a substantial portion of the land was requisitioned by the Government for military purposes during the Second World War.",
      "The company informed the purchaser that the agreement stood cancelled because performance had become impossible.",
      "Satyabrata challenged the cancellation before the Supreme Court."
    ],
    "decision": [
      "The Supreme Court held that the contract had not become impossible to perform merely because the Government had temporarily requisitioned a portion of the land.",
      "The requisition was temporary in nature and did not destroy the fundamental basis of the contract.",
      "The Court explained that the word “impossible” under Section 56 does not mean only literal or physical impossibility.",
      "Performance may also become impossible where an unexpected event destroys the fundamental purpose of the contract.",
      "However, every difficulty, delay or temporary interruption does not automatically frustrate a contract.",
      "The Court held that the contract was not frustrated in the circumstances of this case.",
      "The judgment is a landmark authority on the doctrine of frustration and subsequent impossibility under Indian law."
    ],
